{#-
  HELPER ENTITIES for piston variables that must survive between automations.

  This is HOME ASSISTANT CONFIG SCHEMA — helper domain names, and the fields
  each domain requires — so it lives here rather than in Python. If HA renames
  a field or adds a required one, a user fixes it in this file with nobody
  upstream (COMPILER_SPEC "Jinja2 everywhere", scoped by Jeremy 2026-08-01:
  anything tracking HA's moving target must be editable).

  Rendered into a PACKAGES folder, not a bare `input_boolean:` include. A
  package MERGES with the user's configuration, so an install that already
  defines its own helpers is not broken by a duplicate key — which would stop
  Home Assistant starting, and would do it at their next restart rather than
  at our compile.

  Input:
    helpers  [{domain, object_id, name, config}] already grouped and sorted

  The numeric bounds come from VARIABLES_SPEC §7.3: input_number REQUIRES min
  and max, webCoRE numbers are unbounded, and these sit inside the range where
  integers stay exactly representable in a 64-bit float so nothing rounds
  silently. The 255 on input_text is the cap on ANY entity state (§7.2), not a
  property of that helper — stated here so the limit is visible where the
  helper is defined.
-#}
# Generated by PistonCore — piston variables that must outlive a single
# automation run. DO NOT EDIT: rewritten on every deploy.
#
# A webCoRE variable read outside the statement that wrote it cannot live in an
# automation's `variables:` block, because PistonCore compiles each statement
# into its own automation. These entities are where those variables live.
#
# Named pistoncore_<piston>_<variable> so you can always tell which piston owns
# one. Deleting a piston removes its helpers.
{% for domain, entries in helpers | groupby('domain') %}
{{ domain }}:
{%- for h in entries | sort(attribute='object_id') %}
  {{ h.object_id }}:
    name: {{ h.name | tojson }}
{%- for key, value in h.config.items() %}
    {{ key }}: {{ value | tojson }}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endfor %}
{% endfor %}
